<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad I Bey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad Bey of Tunis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad I Bey was the Husainid bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> from 1837 to 1855. He modernised the army on European lines, founded a military school at Bardo and built factories, and abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in 1846, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The cost of his reforms burdened the treasury and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>'s later debts.</p></div>
